Q: Auto-generated GRPC classes aren&#39;t accessible as source folders in EclipseIDE, but exist A: <p>The fix is to declare the classpath as source-folder before the buildship plugin can overwrite the changes by using <code>file.beforeMerged {}</code> instead of <code>file.whenMerged {}</code>. The following change resolved the issue:</p>
<pre><code>eclipse {
    classpath {
        file.beforeMerged { cp -&gt;
            cp.entries.add(
                new org.gradle.plugins.ide.eclipse.model.SourceFolder(
                    'build/generated/source/proto/main/grpc', null))
            cp.entries.add(
                new org.gradle.plugins.ide.eclipse.model.SourceFolder(
                    'build/generated/source/proto/main/java', null))
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: playwright - get content from multiple pages in parallel A: <p>The problem is <code>for (url in item.urls) {</code> rather than <code>for (url of item.urls) {</code> (ideally use <code>const url</code> to avoid a global). See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/29285897/difference-between-for-in-and-for-of-statements">Difference between ( for... in ) and ( for... of ) statements?</a>.</p>
<p>If you log <code>url</code> in your original code, you'll see it's <code>&quot;0&quot;, &quot;1&quot;, &quot;2&quot;...</code> rather than URLs as you expect.</p>
<p>I'm not sure what version of Playwright you were using when this was asked, but on 1.57.0, the error is much clearer:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>node:internal/modules/run_main:122
    triggerUncaughtException(
    ^

page.goto: Protocol error (Page.navigate): Invalid url: &quot;0&quot;
Call log:
  - navigating to &quot;0&quot;, waiting until &quot;domcontentloaded&quot;

    at getContent (/home/g/programming/scraping/a.js:8:16)
    at async file:///home/g/programming/scraping/a.js:25:21
    at async file:///home/g/programming/scraping/a.js:20:16 {
  name: 'Error'
}

Node.js v22.13.0
</code></pre>
<p>Here's the fix, fully runnable:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import {firefox} from &quot;playwright&quot;;

const getContent = async url =&gt; {
  let browser;
  try {
    browser = await firefox.launch({headless: true});
    const page = await browser.newPage();
    await page.goto(url, {waitUntil: &quot;domcontentloaded&quot;});
    return await page.content();
  } finally {
    await browser?.close();
  }
};

const items = [
  {urls: [&quot;https://www.google.com&quot;, &quot;https://www.example.com&quot;]},
  {urls: [&quot;https://www.google.com&quot;, &quot;https://www.example.com&quot;]},
];

const result = await Promise.all(
  items.map(async item =&gt; {
    const contents = [];

    for (const url of item.urls) {
      contents.push(await getContent(url));
    }

    return contents;
  })
);
console.log(result);
</code></pre>
<p>If <code>items</code> is large, a massive <code>Promise.all</code> can cause issues. Use <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/p-limit" rel="nofollow noreferrer">p-limit</a> to ensure no more than a few browsers are live at a given time.</p>
<p>BTW, <code>const content = getContent('https://www.example.com');</code> wouldn't work on its own, since it's missing an <code>await</code>.</p>
<p>Note also that <code>&quot;domcontentloaded&quot;</code> might not make sense for this use case, since it more or less captures the same thing you'd see from fetching the HTML source (assuming bot detection isn't a factor). Something like <code>&quot;load&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;networkidle&quot;</code> would give the page more of a chance to fill out with dynamic JS content coming in from requests. See <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/52497252/puppeteer-wait-until-page-is-completely-loaded/72535642#72535642">this answer</a> for a deep dive into page loading.</p>
